{"records":[{"id":"vffmxyn7cy","title":"Original western blots","description":"The Original western blots of “Sappanchalcone Suppresses Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer Cells by Oxidative Stress-Driven DNA Damage and ER Stress Activation through PIEZO1 Modulation”","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-17T14:46:07.044Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vffmxyn7cy","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vffmxyn7cy"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"WeiYu","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65spg2k8z6","title":"Synthesis and mechanism study of a new bi-gold compound targeting mitochondria to eliminate cancer stem cells","description":"Our work demonstrate that the increase of positive charge could generally gain anticancer potency for gold-containing compounds. The bi-gold series compounds (series 2) generally exhibited better potency to inhibit cancer cells. The best compound BGC2a was accumulated preferentially within mitochondria, leading to increase of ROS and disruption of mitochondrial function. BGC2a was more effective in lung cancer cells with high stem-like side population and glioblastoma stem cells. In-vivo animal study confirmed that BGC2a effectively suppressed tumor growth of lung cancer xenograft, which is also validated in the pancreatic cancer xenograft model.\nThe raw data associated with the figures in manuscript are contained. Of note, some of them are in .pzfx format that can be viewed by GraphPad software (V6 and above). NMR spectra of the synthetic compounds are in .mnova format that can be viewed by MestReNova software.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-15T03:33:01.687Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65spg2k8z6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65spg2k8z6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"shijun","last_name":"wen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitochondrion","Cancer Stem Cell","Gold"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6jbv72m5zv","title":"Proteomic Data of Hedyosmum brasiliense Essential Oil Against Breast Cancer Cells","description":"This dataset contains raw proteomic data derived from label-free quantitative mass spectrometry (shotgun proteomics) of human breast epithelial cells (MCF-10A) and triple-negative breast cancer cells (MDA-MB-231), either untreated or treated with Hedyosmum brasiliense essential oil. Data are provided in Excel format and include protein identifiers, quantification values, fold changes, and statistical significance (p-values). The files are organized by cell type comparison:\n\nPTN_TABLE_HB10A x CTRL10A.xlsx – Proteomic data comparing treated and control MCF-10A cells\n\nPTN_TABLE_HBMDA x CTRLMDA.xlsx – Proteomic data comparing treated and control MDA-MB-231 cells\n\nThese data support the manuscript: Hedyosmum brasiliense Essential Oil Induces Necrotic Cell Death in Triple-Negative Breast Cancer via Tubulin Destabilization and Stress Pathway Dysregulation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-24T15:20:45.194Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6jbv72m5zv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6jbv72m5zv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fabiana","last_name":"Barcelos Furtado","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Geysson Javier ","last_name":"Fernandez","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","grant_id":"2015/14278-6"}],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2d8wzkcn3v","title":"CELL-CHEMICAL-BIOLOGY-D-17-00243 Shimada et al.","description":"This dataset contains i) metabolomic profiling performed by Metabolon, ii) FPKM-normalized RNA-seq data performed by the Stockwell lab and Columbia Genome Center, and iii) 83 compounds identified to induce similar mechanism of lethality in cancer cell lines, for the manuscript \"Copper-binding small molecule induces oxidative stress and cell cycle arrest in glioblastoma-patient-derived cells\" written by Shimada et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-25T23:28:55.694Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2d8wzkcn3v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2d8wzkcn3v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kenichi","last_name":"Shimada","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolomics","RNA Sequencing","Chemical Compound"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"x29rjbpk8r","title":"Raw Data for \"A chemical screen identifies PRMT5 as a therapeutic vulnerability for paclitaxel-resistant triple-negative breast cancer\"","description":"This file contains the following raw data for \"A chemical screen identifies PRMT5 as a therapeutic vulnerability for paclitaxel-resistant triple-negative breast cancer\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-24T09:06:29.878Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/x29rjbpk8r","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/x29rjbpk8r"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Juan","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sheyu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biologicals"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tn42tykb2z","title":"Supplemental data for Early life oxidant pollutant exposure induces lung redox and RAAS dysregulation: Implications for innate immune responses","description":"Supplemental data for Early life oxidant pollutant exposure induces lung redox and RAAS dysregulation: Implications for innate immune responses to respiratory infections ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-31T13:39:31.052Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tn42tykb2z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tn42tykb2z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Janice","last_name":"Dye","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ozone"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ngttmkkhkn","title":"ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer","description":"Ferroptosis presents great potential for cancer therapy, either alone or in combination with classical therapy. However, inducing ferroptosis by targeting canonical ferroptosis suppressors that directly inhibit lipid peroxidation non-selectively induces ferroptosis in both cancerous and normal cells, thereby limiting its therapeutic potential. In this study, we reveal that aldolase A (ALDOA) reprograms lipid metabolism to resist ferroptosis in cancer cells and identify ALDOA as a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization. Cancer cells with ALDOA suppression exhibit increased susceptibility to ferroptosis-a response less obvious in normal cells. Mechanistically, ALDOA depletion induces significant accumulation of fructose 1,6-bisphosphate in cancer cells, thereby enhancing autophagy-dependent degradation of phospholipid-modifying enzymes. These alterations increase the ratio of phospholipids containing pro-ferroptotic polyunsaturated fatty acids over anti-ferroptotic monounsaturated fatty acids, culminating in heightened ferroptosis sensitivity. Moreover, ALDOA inhibitors selectively promote ferroptosis in cancer cells, both in vitro and in vivo. Collectively, our findings reveal that ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-27T01:37:28.511Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ngttmkkhkn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ngttmkkhkn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"pengqi","last_name":"wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"ding","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Tsinghua-Peking Center for Life Sciences","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"Beijing Natural Science Foundation","grant_id":"JQ22016"},{"id":null,"name":"New Cornerstone Investigator Program","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"National Key R&D Program of China ","grant_id":"2022YFA1103704"}],"categories":["Lipidomics","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dy97pwyf74","title":"The hyaluronidase, TMEM2, promotes ER homeostasis and longevity independent of the UPRER","description":"Cells have evolved complex mechanisms to maintain protein homeostasis, such as the UPRER, that are strongly associated with several diseases and the aging process. We performed a whole genome CRISPR-based knockout screen to identify genes important for cells to survive ER-based protein misfolding stress. We identified the cell-surface hyaluronidase, Transmembrane Protein 2 (TMEM2), as a potent modulator of ER-stress resistance. The breakdown of the glycosaminoglycan, hyaluronan (HA), by TMEM2 within the extracellular matrix (ECM) altered ER stress resistance independent of canonical UPRER pathways, but dependent upon the cell surface receptor, CD44, a putative HA receptor, and the MAPK cell-signaling components, ERK and p38. Lastly, and most surprisingly, ectopic expression of human TMEM2 in C. elegans protected animals from ER stress and increased both longevity and pathogen resistance independent of canonical UPRER activation, but dependent on the ERK ortholog, mpk-1, and the p38 ortholog, pmk-1. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-24T15:51:23.538Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dy97pwyf74","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dy97pwyf74"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ryo","last_name":"Higuchi-Sanabria","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert","last_name":"Schinzel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ophir","last_name":"Shalem","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transcriptome"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7y2bm72bgm","title":"Carvacrol alleviates endoplasmic reticulum stress and inflammation induced by lipopolysaccharide by enhancing endoplasmic reticulum autophagy in dairy mammary epithelial cells.","description":"Carvacrol (CAV) exhibits the capacity to augment endoplasmic reticulum autophagy (ER-phagy) in bovine mammary epithelial cells, mitigating endoplasmic reticulum stress (ER-stress) and inflammatory responses, thereby contributing positively to the amelioration of bovine mastitis. By Yang et al. In this paper, the effect of natural plant monomer CAV on enhancing ER-phagy and alleviating ER-stress and inflammation in mammary epithelial cells of dairy cows was discussed in the treatment of mastitis. Through in vivo and in vitro experiments, we found that ER-stress in mastitis tissue was significantly up-regulated, and the ER-phagy pathway, a key pathway that regulates cell homeostasis, was impaired. And CAV was proved to enhance endoplasmic reticulum autophagy. We found that there is a correlation between ER-phagy and ER-stress in dairy mammary epithelial cells, which may be an important direction for the treatment of dairy mastitis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-31T04:50:03.438Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7y2bm72bgm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7y2bm72bgm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yue","last_name":"yang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Dairy Cattle"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6kntht4s2z","title":"Cancer Cell Cytotoxicity and Viral DNA Replication Inhibition Data ","description":"Chemical Compounds Characterization data:  NMR and HRMS\nNMR raw data \nMTT assay description and representative graphs \nSRB assay in 60 cancer cell lines for selected compounds\nAnti-viral screening (including cytotoxicity in host cells)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-26T10:16:39.674Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6kntht4s2z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6kntht4s2z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Vladislav","last_name":"Litosh","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chemistry","Mass Spectrometry","Nuclear Magnetic Resonance","Bioassay"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":133662,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":161,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":133662,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132926,"restricted":68,"embargoed":668},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2251},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":676},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":469},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":403},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":331},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":268},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":224}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":131030},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1902},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0